{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789463588,"nonce":"kDQ4spKPggLnEP1gveQHCE0lLu4=","title":"Compile time: a Python program compiled its whole reachable library up front","message":"The interpreter rows have no compile column, so total = compile + exec is the number to beat, and compile was 80 to 90 ms on the data kernels against PyPy's 65 to 118 ms of exec. The breakdown on bench_tokenize (ZYNTAX_TRACE_OPT_PHASES=1, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES=1): parse 4 ms, lower 10 ms (lower_decls 6 ms over 509 declarations), optimiser 40 ms (only 11 functions unoptimised; drop_insert 8, licm+cse 8, inline 7, licm 5, most of it on main after inlining), Cranelift codegen 30 ms for 131 functions of which about 25 are ever called.\n\nLanded: a snapshot function that only a direct call reaches is compiled on its first call, from its already-optimised body (`callable_only_function_ids`, the `finished` set handed to the lazy compiler), so codegen covers what runs. The address-taken walk over the module ran on every first-call compile and now runs once per module. Interleaved A/B, median of 5, eager vs lazy library: tokenize compile 85 to 65 ms, run 140 to 127; pipeline 77 to 58, run 65 to 76; records 83 to 63, run 387 to 396. Totals improve by 8 to 33 ms. `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LAZY_LIBRARY=1` restores the eager compile.\n\nStill to take, in order of size:\n- First-call compiles wait on the Cranelift backend lock while a background promotion compiles main at tier 1 (a 9 ms wait on a 17-instruction function was measured; `ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY=1` prints each first-call compile with the wait included). Cranelift's own compile could run outside the lock: translate to CLIF under it, `Context::compile` outside, `define_function_bytes` under it again.\n- HIR to CLIF translation runs at about 5 us per instruction (3.6 ms for a 680-instruction function against Cranelift's own 2.4 ms); a profile of compile_function_body is owed.\n- The boxes pass un-marks every snapshot function it touches, so licm+cse then walk all 131 (8 ms); with the library lazy those passes belong in the first-call path for the functions it compiles.\n- lower_decls spends 6 ms on 509 declarations of a prelowered snapshot; parse spends 4 ms on a 20-line file.\n- The structural answer is machine code for the library shipped with the frontend (Cranelift to an object file at build time, calls into JIT code through cells), which takes library codegen out of every run and removes the contention with it.","files":null}]}